.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt PTRACE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ptrace
.Nd trace a process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/ptrace.h
.Ft long
.Fn ptrace "int request" "pid_t pid" "void *addr" "void *data"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm ,
number 26, lets one process, the tracer, stop the threads of another, its
tracees, read and change their memory and registers, and resume them, as
GDB and strace do.
.Fa pid
is a thread ID.
The tracer is a process: any thread of it may make the requests and wait
for the tracees.
.Pp
A tracee stops for its tracer in a
.Em ptrace stop ,
which the tracer learns of from
.Xr wait 2
with the status
.Dv WIFSTOPPED ,
and from a
.Dv SIGCHLD
with
.Dv CLD_TRAPPED .
The tracer's wait reports each stop once, and reports a traced thread that
is not the first of its process only with
.Dv __WALL
or
.Dv __WCLONE .
The end of a traced process whose parent is another process is reported
to the tracer first, and the report lets the process go: its parent then
receives
.Dv SIGCHLD
and may reap it.
A tracer that ends lets its tracees go, and the parent of one whose
process has ended is told of the end the same way.
The stops are:
.Bl -tag -width "signal-delivery stop"
.It signal-delivery stop
A signal the tracee is about to act on, other than
.Dv SIGKILL ,
with the signal in the status; the signal the tracer resumes it with is
the one acted on, 0 for none, and a blocked one stays pending.
.It group stop
The stop of the tracee's process by a stop signal, after the
signal-delivery stop for it; the tracer may resume the thread while the
process stays stopped.
With
.Dv PTRACE_SEIZE
it is reported with
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_STOP
in bits 16 to 23 of the status.
.It event stop
.Dv SIGTRAP
with an event in bits 16 to 23, when the options ask for it:
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_FORK ,
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_VFORK
and
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_CLONE
after a
.Xr fork 2 ,
a
.Xr vfork 2
or
.Xr clone 2
by the tracee, whose new child or thread is then traced too and first
stops with
.Dv SIGSTOP ,
or
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_STOP
under
.Dv PTRACE_SEIZE ;
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_VFORK_DONE
when the vfork child lets the tracee go;
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_EXEC
after an
.Xr execve 2 ;
and
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_EXIT
as the thread begins to end.
.Dv PTRACE_GETEVENTMSG
reads the new child's ID, the thread's ID before the exec, or the exit
status.
Without
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACEEXEC ,
an
.Xr execve 2
sends the tracee
.Dv SIGTRAP .
.It syscall stop
At the entry and at the exit of each system call, after
.Dv PTRACE_SYSCALL ,
with
.Dv SIGTRAP ,
or
.Dv SIGTRAP No | 0x80
with
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACESYSGOOD .
At the entry EAX holds
.Er -ENOSYS
and orig_eax the call's number; at the exit EAX holds the result.
.El
.Pp
The requests are:
.Bl -tag -width "PTRACE_GET_THREAD_AREA"
.It Dv PTRACE_TRACEME
The caller's parent becomes its tracer.
.It Dv PTRACE_ATTACH , PTRACE_SEIZE
The caller's process becomes the tracer of
.Fa pid .
.Dv PTRACE_ATTACH
sends it
.Dv SIGSTOP ;
.Dv PTRACE_SEIZE
stops nothing and takes the options in
.Fa data .
The caller needs effective user ID 0, or a real user and group ID equal to
each of the target's three and a dumpable target, as Linux asks of a
tracer without
.Dv CAP_SYS_PTRACE .
A thread of the caller's own process, init, a kernel thread and a thread
already traced cannot be attached.
.It Dv PTRACE_PEEKTEXT , PTRACE_PEEKDATA , PTRACE_POKETEXT , PTRACE_POKEDATA
Read the word at
.Fa addr
of the tracee's memory into the word
.Fa data
points to, or write
.Fa data
there.
Missing pages are brought in, and a write reaches a read-only private page,
such as program text, through a copy of its own, so a breakpoint changes
this process alone; a page of no mapping, of a
.Dv PROT_NONE
one, or of a read-only shared one for a write, is
.Er EIO .
.It Dv PTRACE_PEEKUSER , PTRACE_POKEUSER
A word of the i386
.Vt struct user
at byte
.Fa addr :
the registers of
.Vt user_regs_struct
from 0, the debug registers
.Va u_debugreg
from 252; the floating-point valid mark reads 1 and the other fields 0.
.It Dv PTRACE_GETREGS , PTRACE_SETREGS
The 68-byte
.Vt user_regs_struct
at
.Fa data .
A tracer changes only the flags a program may change; the data segment
registers take a null selector, the user data selector or the TLS entry's,
and CS and SS only the ones they have.
An orig_eax of -1 keeps an interrupted system call from being restarted.
.It Dv PTRACE_GETFPREGS , PTRACE_SETFPREGS
The 108-byte
.Vt user_i387_struct .
.It Dv PTRACE_GETFPXREGS , PTRACE_SETFPXREGS
The 512-byte
.Ic fxsave
image;
.Er EIO
on a processor without FXSR, and
.Er EINVAL
for an MXCSR the processor refuses.
.It Dv PTRACE_CONT , PTRACE_SYSCALL , PTRACE_SINGLESTEP
Resume the tracee with the signal
.Fa data ,
0 for none; after a stop that is not a signal-delivery stop the signal is
sent afresh after a syscall stop and dropped after any other.
.Dv PTRACE_SYSCALL
stops it at the next system call's entry or exit, and
.Dv PTRACE_SINGLESTEP
after one instruction, with
.Dv SIGTRAP
and
.Dv TRAP_TRACE .
.It Dv PTRACE_KILL
Send the tracee
.Dv SIGKILL .
.It Dv PTRACE_DETACH
Resume the tracee with the signal
.Fa data
and stop tracing it; its debug registers are cleared.
.It Dv PTRACE_INTERRUPT
Stop a tracee attached with
.Dv PTRACE_SEIZE
with
.Dv PTRACE_EVENT_STOP
and
.Dv SIGTRAP ,
at its next return to user mode.
.It Dv PTRACE_LISTEN
Accepted for a seized tracee, which stays stopped.
.It Dv PTRACE_SETOPTIONS
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACESYSGOOD ,
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACEFORK ,
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACEVFORK ,
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACECLONE ,
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACEEXEC ,
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACEVFORKDONE ,
.Dv PTRACE_O_TRACEEXIT
and
.Dv PTRACE_O_EXITKILL ,
which sends the tracees
.Dv SIGKILL
when the tracer ends; every tracee is detached then.
.It Dv PTRACE_GETEVENTMSG
The event's message, into the word at
.Fa data .
.It Dv PTRACE_GETSIGINFO , PTRACE_SETSIGINFO
The 128-byte
.Vt siginfo_t
of the stop.
.It Dv PTRACE_GET_THREAD_AREA , PTRACE_SET_THREAD_AREA
The TLS descriptor of entry
.Fa addr ,
which must be 6, the one entry, as a
.Vt user_desc ,
as
.Xr set_thread_area 2
describes it.
.El
.Pp
Every request but
.Dv PTRACE_TRACEME ,
.Dv PTRACE_ATTACH ,
.Dv PTRACE_SEIZE ,
.Dv PTRACE_KILL
and
.Dv PTRACE_INTERRUPT
needs the tracee in a ptrace stop.
.Ss Debug registers
DR0 to DR3 hold breakpoint addresses, DR6 the status of the last debug
exception, and DR7 the control bits, which the kernel loads when the
tracee runs.
DR7 may enable a breakpoint only on a user address aligned to its length
of 1, 2 or 4 bytes, an execute breakpoint only of length 1, and neither an
I/O breakpoint nor the general-detect bit; a value against these is
.Er EINVAL .
A breakpoint that fires sends
.Dv SIGTRAP
with
.Dv TRAP_HWBKPT ,
also when the kernel touches the address in a copy for the tracee.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
0, or the negative error number of the raw call; the C libraries' wrappers
return -1 and set
.Va errno ,
and return the word for the three peek requests.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Pa tests/musl/ptracemusl.c
traces a child that stops itself, reads and writes its memory directly and
through
.Pa /proc/PID/mem ,
reads its registers and floating-point state, steps an instruction, stops
it at a breakpoint in its read-only text and moves it back, stops it at
the entry and exit of a system call and after an
.Xr execve 2 ,
sets and reads a debug register, and attaches to and detaches from a
running process.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A buffer at
.Fa data
cannot be read or written.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
A debug register, an MXCSR, a TLS descriptor or entry, or an option the
kernel does not take.
.It Bq Er EIO
An unknown request, a
.Vt struct user
offset out of range or not a word's, a register value the kernel refuses,
a signal past 64, a memory address no mapping allows, or
.Dv PTRACE_INTERRUPT
or
.Dv PTRACE_LISTEN
for a tracee that was not seized.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The attach is not allowed, or the caller is already traced.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
No such thread, one the caller's process does not trace, or one not in a
ptrace stop.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr set_thread_area 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2 ,
.Xr wait 2
